  "answer": "The largest living animal species that only eats plants is the African bush elephant (*Loxodonta africana*).",  
  "explanation": "African bush elephants are the largest terrestrial animals alive today, with males weighing up to 6,000–7,000 kg (13,000–15,000 lb) and standing up to 4 meters (13 ft) tall at the shoulder. They are strict herbivores, consuming grasses, leaves, bark, fruits, and roots. No larger living herbivore exists: marine herbivores like manatees or dugongs are far smaller (typically <500 kg), and other large land herbivores (e.g., white rhinos, hippos) max out at under 2,500 kg. While extinct sauropod dinosaurs were larger, they do not qualify as 'living' species. Blue whales, though larger overall, are carnivorous (feeding on krill) and not herbivorous."  
